Method2:Java Solution (In‑Place Style)
class Solution {
    public String reverseWords(String s) {
        // Step 1: Remove leading and trailing spaces so we don't process empty words at the edges
        s = s.trim();
        
        // Step 2: Create a StringBuilder to build the final reversed string efficiently
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        
        // Step 3: Initialize a pointer 'end' at the last character of the trimmed string
        int end = s.length() - 1;
        int start; // 'start' will mark the beginning of each word
        
        // Step 4: Loop until we've scanned the entire string backwards
        while (end >= 0) {
            // Step 4a: Skip any spaces we encounter (move 'end' left until we hit a letter)
            while (end >= 0 && s.charAt(end) == ' ') {
                end--;
            }
            // Step 4b: If we've moved past the beginning, stop the loop
            if (end < 0) break;
            
            // Step 4c: Find the start index of the current word
            start = end;
            while (start >= 0 && s.charAt(start) != ' ') {
                start--;
            }
            
            // Step 4d: Extract the word using substring (from start+1 to end inclusive)
            sb.append(s.substring(start + 1, end + 1));
            
            // Step 4e: If there are more words left, add a space after this word
            if (start > 0) {
                sb.append(" ");
            }
            
            // Step 4f: Move 'end' pointer to just before this word to continue scanning
            end = start;
        }
        
        // Step 5: Convert the StringBuilder into a String and return the result
        return sb.toString();
    }
}


Time Complexity: O(n) (each character scanned once).
Space Complexity: O(n) (for the result string).
